The procedure below assumes `@P__RawTokenHash` contains the 64-character lowercase SHA-256 hash produced by C#—not the raw Base64Url token. SQL compares it directly with `t_DocumentReference.TokenHash`.

It validates the document, policy, lifetime rules, rolling-window rules, records the audit event, and returns `DocumentCuk` only when access is authorized.

```sql
USE [Alterna_AccountStatementManagement]
GO

CREATE PROCEDURE [dbo].[p_ValidateDocumentAccess]
(
    @P__RawTokenHash NVARCHAR(255),
    @P__Error        NVARCHAR(4000) OUTPUT
)
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;
    SET XACT_ABORT ON;

    DECLARE @NowUtc DATETIME2(3) = SYSUTCDATETIME();

    DECLARE @TokenHash NVARCHAR(255) =
        LOWER(LTRIM(RTRIM(ISNULL(@P__RawTokenHash, N''))));

    DECLARE @CorrelationId NVARCHAR(255) =
        CONVERT(NVARCHAR(36), NEWID());

    DECLARE @DocumentReferenceId BIGINT = NULL;
    DECLARE @AccessPolicyId BIGINT = NULL;
    DECLARE @DocumentCuk NVARCHAR(255) = NULL;
    DECLARE @ExpiredDate DATETIME2(3) = NULL;
    DECLARE @RevokedDate DATETIME2(3) = NULL;
    DECLARE @DocumentIsActive BIT = NULL;
    DECLARE @PolicyIsEnabled BIT = NULL;

    DECLARE @Allowed BIT = 0;
    DECLARE @ConsumesQuota BIT = 0;
    DECLARE @OutcomeCode NVARCHAR(255) = N'UNDETERMINED';

    DECLARE @DeniedRuleId BIGINT = NULL;
    DECLARE @DeniedRuleName NVARCHAR(255) = NULL;
    DECLARE @CurrentAccessCount BIGINT = NULL;
    DECLARE @MaximumAccessCount INT = NULL;
    DECLARE @WindowSeconds INT = NULL;

    SET @P__Error = NULL;

    BEGIN TRY
        BEGIN TRANSACTION;

        /*
         * Expected format:
         * 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters representing SHA-256.
         */
        IF LEN(@TokenHash) <> 64
           OR @TokenHash COLLATE Latin1_General_100_BIN2
                LIKE N'%[^0-9a-f]%'
        BEGIN
            SET @OutcomeCode = N'INVALID_TOKEN_FORMAT';
            SET @P__Error = N'The document token is invalid.';
        END
        ELSE
        BEGIN
            /*
             * UPDLOCK and HOLDLOCK serialize access attempts for the
             * same document so concurrent scans cannot both pass
             * the quota check.
             */
            SELECT
                @DocumentReferenceId = d.Id,
                @AccessPolicyId = d.AccessPolicyId,
                @DocumentCuk = d.DocumentCuk,
                @ExpiredDate = d.ExpiredDate,
                @RevokedDate = d.RevokedDate,
                @DocumentIsActive = d.IsActive,
                @PolicyIsEnabled = p.IsEnabled
            FROM dbo.t_DocumentReference AS d
                WITH (UPDLOCK, HOLDLOCK)
            INNER JOIN dbo.t_AccessPolicy AS p
                WITH (HOLDLOCK)
                ON p.Id = d.AccessPolicyId
            WHERE d.TokenHash = @TokenHash;

            IF @DocumentReferenceId IS NULL
            BEGIN
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'INVALID_TOKEN';
                SET @P__Error = N'The document token is invalid.';
            END
            ELSE IF @DocumentIsActive = 0
            BEGIN
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'DOCUMENT_INACTIVE';
                SET @P__Error = N'The requested document is not available.';
            END
            ELSE IF @RevokedDate IS NOT NULL
            BEGIN
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'DOCUMENT_REVOKED';
                SET @P__Error = N'Document access has been revoked.';
            END
            ELSE IF @NowUtc >= @ExpiredDate
            BEGIN
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'DOCUMENT_EXPIRED';
                SET @P__Error = N'Document access has expired.';
            END
            ELSE IF ISNULL(@PolicyIsEnabled, 0) = 0
            BEGIN
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'POLICY_DISABLED';
                SET @P__Error = N'The document access policy is disabled.';
            END
            ELSE IF NOT EXISTS
            (
                SELECT 1
                FROM dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule AS r
                    WITH (HOLDLOCK)
                WHERE r.AccessPolicyId = @AccessPolicyId
            )
            BEGIN
                /*
                 * Fail closed if the policy has no rules.
                 */
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'POLICY_RULE_NOT_CONFIGURED';
                SET @P__Error =
                    N'No access rules are configured for this policy.';
            END
            ELSE IF EXISTS
            (
                SELECT 1
                FROM dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule AS r
                    WITH (HOLDLOCK)
                WHERE r.AccessPolicyId = @AccessPolicyId
                  AND
                  (
                      r.MaximumAccessCount <= 0
                      OR
                      (
                          r.WindowSeconds IS NOT NULL
                          AND r.WindowSeconds <= 0
                      )
                  )
            )
            BEGIN
                SET @OutcomeCode = N'POLICY_CONFIGURATION_INVALID';
                SET @P__Error =
                    N'The document access policy contains an invalid rule.';
            END
            ELSE
            BEGIN
                /*
                 * WindowSeconds IS NULL:
                 *     Count every quota-consuming access for the lifetime
                 *     of the document reference.
                 *
                 * WindowSeconds has a value:
                 *     Count accesses inside the rolling time window.
                 */
                SELECT TOP (1)
                    @DeniedRuleId = r.Id,
                    @DeniedRuleName = r.RuleName,
                    @WindowSeconds = r.WindowSeconds,
                    @MaximumAccessCount = r.MaximumAccessCount,
                    @CurrentAccessCount = q.CurrentAccessCount
                FROM dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule AS r
                    WITH (HOLDLOCK)
                CROSS APPLY
                (
                    SELECT
                        COUNT_BIG(1) AS CurrentAccessCount
                    FROM dbo.t_DocumentAccessAudit AS a
                    WHERE a.DocumentReferenceId =
                              @DocumentReferenceId
                      AND a.ConsumesQuota = 1
                      AND
                      (
                          r.WindowSeconds IS NULL
                          OR a.CreatedDate >
                             DATEADD(
                                 SECOND,
                                 -r.WindowSeconds,
                                 @NowUtc
                             )
                      )
                      AND a.CreatedDate <= @NowUtc
                ) AS q
                WHERE r.AccessPolicyId = @AccessPolicyId
                  AND q.CurrentAccessCount >=
                      r.MaximumAccessCount
                ORDER BY
                    CASE
                        WHEN r.WindowSeconds IS NULL THEN 1
                        ELSE 0
                    END,
                    r.WindowSeconds,
                    r.Id;

                IF @DeniedRuleId IS NOT NULL
                BEGIN
                    SET @OutcomeCode = N'ACCESS_LIMIT_EXCEEDED';

                    SET @P__Error =
                        LEFT(
                            CONCAT(
                                N'Access limit exceeded. Rule: ',
                                @DeniedRuleName,
                                N'; current count: ',
                                @CurrentAccessCount,
                                N'; maximum: ',
                                @MaximumAccessCount,
                                CASE
                                    WHEN @WindowSeconds IS NULL
                                    THEN N'; window: lifetime.'
                                    ELSE CONCAT(
                                        N'; window seconds: ',
                                        @WindowSeconds,
                                        N'.'
                                    )
                                END
                            ),
                            4000
                        );
                END
                ELSE
                BEGIN
                    SET @Allowed = 1;
                    SET @ConsumesQuota = 1;
                    SET @OutcomeCode = N'ACCESS_GRANTED';
                    SET @P__Error = NULL;
                END
            END
        END

        /*
         * Every known validation attempt is recorded.
         * Only ACCESS_GRANTED consumes quota.
         */
        INSERT INTO dbo.t_DocumentAccessAudit
        (
            DocumentReferenceId,
            CorrelationId,
            PresentedTokenHash,
            OutcomeCode,
            ConsumesQuota,
            DeniedByRuleId,
            IpAddress,
            UserAgent,
            CreatedDate,
            CreatedBy
        )
        VALUES
        (
            @DocumentReferenceId,
            @CorrelationId,
            @TokenHash,
            @OutcomeCode,
            @ConsumesQuota,
            @DeniedRuleId,
            NULL,
            NULL,
            @NowUtc,
            N'AlternaSystem'
        );

        COMMIT TRANSACTION;

        IF @Allowed = 1
        BEGIN
            SELECT @DocumentCuk AS DocumentCuk;
            RETURN 0;
        END;

        SELECT CAST(NULL AS NVARCHAR(255)) AS DocumentCuk;
        RETURN 1;
    END TRY
    BEGIN CATCH
        IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0
        BEGIN
            ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
        END;

        /*
         * This output should be logged internally.
         * Do not return the SQL error directly to the public user.
         */
        SET @P__Error =
            LEFT(
                CONCAT(
                    N'Unexpected error while validating document access: ',
                    ERROR_MESSAGE()
                ),
                4000
            );

        SELECT CAST(NULL AS NVARCHAR(255)) AS DocumentCuk;
        RETURN -1;
    END CATCH;
END
GO
```

## How it evaluates the rules

For this rule:

```text
WindowSeconds = 180
MaximumAccessCount = 2
```

The procedure counts:

```sql
ConsumesQuota = 1
AND CreatedDate > DATEADD(SECOND, -180, @NowUtc)
```

If the current count is already `2`, access is denied before inserting another quota-consuming audit row.

For a lifetime rule:

```text
WindowSeconds = NULL
MaximumAccessCount = 100
```

It counts every audit row for that document where:

```sql
ConsumesQuota = 1
```

## Required indexes

The token hash must be unique:

```sql
CREATE UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED INDEX
    UX_t_DocumentReference_TokenHash
ON dbo.t_DocumentReference(TokenHash);
GO
```

For fast quota evaluation:

```sql
CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX
    IX_t_DocumentAccessAudit_QuotaWindow
ON dbo.t_DocumentAccessAudit
(
    DocumentReferenceId,
    CreatedDate
)
WHERE ConsumesQuota = 1;
GO
```

For retrieving policy rules:

```sql
CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX
    IX_t_AccessPolicyRule_AccessPolicyId
ON dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule(AccessPolicyId)
INCLUDE
(
    RuleName,
    WindowSeconds,
    MaximumAccessCount
);
GO
```

Add constraints so invalid rules cannot be saved:

```sql
ALTER TABLE dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule
ADD CONSTRAINT CK_t_AccessPolicyRule_MaximumAccessCount
CHECK (MaximumAccessCount > 0);
GO

ALTER TABLE dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule
ADD CONSTRAINT CK_t_AccessPolicyRule_WindowSeconds
CHECK
(
    WindowSeconds IS NULL
    OR WindowSeconds > 0
);
GO
```

Your current `t_AccessPolicyRule` table has no effective-from/effective-to or active columns, so this procedure considers every rule belonging to the policy active.

Also, the procedure uses UTC through `SYSUTCDATETIME()`. Ensure `ExpiredDate` and audit dates are also stored as UTC; otherwise, use one consistent time convention throughout the database.

The returned CUK should remain inside the service layer if it is an internal document-system identifier. The public browser should receive the document or a controlled download response, not the CUK itself.
